<div class="key-takeaways"><h2>Key takeaways</h2><ul>
<li>The NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database lists 841 hail reports for Dallas County since 1955.</li>
<li>Dallas hail season runs March through June. Those months hold about 79% of all reports, and April and May together account for more than half.</li>
<li>250 reports, about three in ten, were 1.75 inches (golf ball size) or larger, and 46 were 2.75 inches (baseball size) or larger.</li>
<li>October is a real second season: 61 reports, more than July and August combined.</li>
<li>2011, 2012, and 2023 were the busiest hail years on record, each with more than 50 reports.</li>
<li>When storms stack up in one season, dated photos and prompt reporting help tie roof damage to the right storm date.</li>
</ul></div>

<p>Ask anyone who has owned a home in Dallas for a few years and you will hear about hail: cracked windshields at NorthPark, a roof replaced twice in five years, a golf course in Las Colinas chewed up the night before a tournament round. North Texas sits in the southern Plains hail belt, and Dallas County gets its share. But when exactly is Dallas hail season, how large does the hail get, and what does any of it mean for an insurance claim?</p>

<p>To answer that with something better than memory, we went to the official record: the NOAA National Centers for Environmental Information (NCEI) Storm Events Database, which logs severe weather reports county by county. Below is what roughly seventy years of Dallas County hail data shows, where the data falls short, and how to use it if your roof is on the line.</p>

<h2>Dallas hail season by month</h2>

<p>The table groups every Dallas County hail report in the NCEI database by month. These are individual reports, not storm days. A single storm crossing the county can produce a dozen reports, so the numbers show when hail tends to cluster rather than how many separate storms occurred.</p>

<table>
