</table>

<p><em>Source: NOAA NCEI Storm Events Database, Dallas County, Texas (county and forecast zone entries), downloaded October 2026. Percentages are rounded.</em></p>

<p>Two things stand out. First, the Dallas season is long. Hail ramps up in March, peaks in April, stays heavy in May, and June is still busier than March. Compared with places farther south, North Texas keeps producing big spring storms deeper into early summer. Second, there is a distinct fall bump. October has more reports than July, August, and November, and nearly twice as many as September. Cold fronts pushing into warm, humid air can still build hail-producing storms well after spring is over.</p>

<p>For planning purposes, the highest-risk stretch runs from mid-March through June, with a second, smaller window in October. Those are the months to have your policy, your deductible, and a set of baseline photos ready.</p>

<h2>How big does hail get in Dallas County?</h2>

<p>Size matters for roofs, skylights, and vehicles, and the Dallas record has plenty of large stones. Of the 841 reports, 606 were at least 1 inch (quarter size), which is the National Weather Service threshold for severe hail. 250 were 1.75 inches (golf ball) or larger, and 46 reached 2.75 inches (baseball) or more. Big hail is more likely to be noticed and reported than small hail, so the share is probably a little inflated. Still, when hail falls here, it is often large enough to cause real damage.</p>

