<p>Note the two February 2000 entries. Giant hail in late winter is unusual, but it is in the record, and it hit two of the most built-up parts of the county.</p>

<h2>The storms Dallas still talks about</h2>

<h3>May 24, 2011: grapefruit hail in Irving</h3>

<p>On May 24, 2011, hail up to the size of grapefruits fell around <a href="/service-areas/irving/">Irving</a> for several minutes. NOAA's narrative describes windows broken or smashed out and serious damage at the TPC Four Seasons Las Colinas, where the Byron Nelson Championship was being played; crews repaired more than 4,000 divots before play resumed. The same afternoon brought baseball-size hail at Skillman Street and Southwestern Boulevard in Dallas, and golf ball hail at Highway 183 and Mockingbird Lane that damaged eight Southwest planes at Love Field.</p>

<h3>June 13, 2012: Lakewood and NorthPark</h3>

<p>About a year later, a storm on June 13, 2012, left one of the most detailed damage descriptions in the county record. NOAA reports that hail up to baseball size caused considerable damage in the Lakewood area of Dallas, leaving holes in some roofs, smashing house and car windows, and shredding trees. The tower at the Lakewood Theater was damaged, and the hail reportedly lasted for nearly 30 minutes. Across town, baseball hail broke skylights at NorthPark Center. The same storm system produced orange-size hail in Grand Prairie and baseball hail on Esters Road in Irving.</p>

<p>Lakewood is a good illustration of local risk. Many homes there are older, some sit on pier-and-beam foundations, and a lot of them have composition shingle roofs, wood trim, and older single-pane windows. Thirty minutes of large hail tests every one of those materials.</p>

<h3>April 3, 2012 and June 12, 2023</h3>

<p>The April 3, 2012 outbreak produced 17 Dallas County reports in one day, including tornadoes and hail up to 3 inches in Coppell. On June 12, 2023, storms on the southwest side dropped hail up to 4 inches near Cedar Hill and baseball hail near Cedar Hill State Park, kicking off a run of June storms that helped make 2023 one of the busiest years on record.</p>

<h3>Recent fall storms</h3>

<p>On September 24, 2023, storms along a front produced 14 hail reports in a single evening, mostly across Richardson and northern <a href="/service-areas/garland/">Garland</a>, with hail up to 2.25 inches. Older October storms are just as notable: on October 2, 1993, hail broke forty windows at Parkland Hospital, and on October 21, 1994, a line of storms dropped hail up to 2.75 inches near Seagoville along with several brief tornado touchdowns.</p>

