<h2>What the data cannot tell you</h2>

<p>The NCEI database is the best long-term public record of hail in Dallas County, but it is not a census of every hailstorm. Keep these limits in mind:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Reports depend on people.</strong> Hail gets logged when a spotter, official, or member of the public reports it to the National Weather Service. A storm at 2 a.m. may hit hundreds of homes and produce only one report.</li>
<li><strong>Reporting has changed.</strong> Smartphone apps such as mPING and social media photos now account for many reports. Older decades look quieter partly because fewer people were reporting.</li>
<li><strong>Location names can mislead.</strong> NCEI uses reference points such as "Gribble" or "Bouchard" for the location field. The event narrative usually names the actual city or intersection.</li>
<li><strong>Sizes are often estimated.</strong> Many reports compare stones to coins or balls rather than measuring them.</li>
</ul>

<p>Even with those caveats, the seasonal pattern is consistent year after year, and it matches what roofers and adjusters see across North Texas.</p>

<h2>What hail season means for your insurance claim</h2>

<h3>Several storms, one roof</h3>

<p>Because hail clusters so tightly between March and June, the same roof can be hit more than once in a season, or several times over a few years. Insurers may then ask which storm caused which damage, or argue that the damage predates the policy. Dated photos, prior repair invoices, and a prompt claim help answer those questions. Our guide to <a href="/document-hail-damage-for-an-insurance-claim/">documenting hail damage for an insurance claim</a> walks through what to capture.</p>

<h3>Your deductible matters more in a busy year</h3>

<p>Many Texas policies carry a separate wind/hail deductible, often 1% or 2% of the dwelling (Coverage A) limit. Dallas County is outside the TWIA coastal area, so Dallas homes usually get wind and hail coverage from their regular insurer. Two separate storms can mean two deductibles, depending on policy wording. Check your declarations page before the season starts.</p>

<h3>Roof settlement terms</h3>

<p>Some Texas policies pay roofs at actual cash value or under an age-based roof payment schedule, and some metal-roof coverage excludes cosmetic damage. In a county with Dallas County's hail record, those terms can affect your outcome as much as the storm itself. Our article on <a href="/replacement-cost-vs-actual-cash-value-texas/">replacement cost vs. actual cash value in Texas</a> explains the difference.</p>

<h3>What size hail damages a roof?</h3>

<p>There is no single cutoff. Damage depends on the roofing material, its age, wind speed and direction during the storm, and the density of the stones. Composition shingles on Dallas brick-veneer homes can bruise or crack at sizes that leave a newer roof untouched. Low-slope TPO and modified-bitumen roofs on apartments and warehouses respond differently again. A slope-by-slope inspection is the only reliable answer; see <a href="/hail-damage-roof-insurance-claim-how-to-protect-your-roof/">hail damage roof insurance claims</a> for what inspectors look for.</p>

<div class="callout"><span class="callout-title">Build a baseline before March</span><p>Late winter is a good time to take dated photos of every roof slope (from the ground or a drone), gutters, vents, AC units, windows, fences, and skylights. Store them with your policy. If a storm hits in April or October, you will have a clear before-and-after record.</p></div>

<h2>What to do after a hailstorm in Dallas</h2>

<ol>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Do not climb onto a wet or damaged roof. Start from the ground, windows, and a second-story vantage point.</li>
<li><strong>Photograph the hail</strong> beside a ruler or coin and note the time.</li>
<li><strong>Check soft metals and exterior items:</strong> gutters, downspouts, roof vents, AC fins, window screens, wood fences, and vehicles.</li>
<li><strong>Look inside</strong> for new ceiling stains, especially after the next rain.</li>
<li><strong>Prevent further damage</strong> with tarps or temporary repairs, and keep every receipt.</li>
<li><strong>Save the storm record</strong> with our <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup</a> or the full <a href="/storm-history/">Dallas County storm history</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Report the claim promptly</strong> and keep a log of calls, emails, and inspection dates. Our <a href="/claim-documentation-checklist/">claim documentation checklist</a> can help.</li>
</ol>

<h2>How a public adjuster can help during hail season</h2>

<p>After a big spring storm, insurers can face a surge of claims, and inspections are sometimes quick. A licensed Texas public adjuster represents the policyholder, not the insurance company. Dallas Public Adjuster, a service of [[company]], helps homeowners and business owners with <a href="/services/hail-damage-claims/">Dallas hail damage claims</a> by inspecting and documenting the damage, matching it to the storm record, preparing a detailed estimate, and presenting the claim to the insurer.</p>

<p>Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Every claim depends on the policy and the facts of the loss, and no outcome is guaranteed. If you are not sure whether a recent storm damaged your property, a Dallas Public Adjuster review is a reasonable first step before you decide what to do.</p>
